87962174, 9780387962177\nLength  | 476 pages\n   |  \nExport Citation  | BiBTeX EndNote RefMan--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nQuotations by Saunders Mac Lane - MacTutor History of Mathematics (https://mathshistory.st-andrews.ac.uk/Biographies/MacLane/quotations/)\nciteturn809search9 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 9 months ago; ### Saunders Mac Lane ... > Mathematics: Form and Function (New York 1986).\n\n# Quotations\n\n### Saunders Mac Lane\n\n> The standard \"foundation\" for mathematics starts with sets and their elements. It is possible to start differently, by axiomatising not elements of sets but functions between sets. This can be done by using the language of categories and universal constructions.\n\n> . . . in one sense a foundation is a security blanket: If you meticulously follow the rules laid down, no paradoxes or contradictions will arise. In reality there is now no guarantee of this sort of security . . .\n\n> . . . the membership relation for sets can often be replaced by the composition operation for functions. This leads to an alternative foundation for Mathematics upon categories -- specifically, on the category of all functions. Now much of Mathematics is dynamic, in that it deals with morphisms of an object into another object of the same kind. Such morphisms (like functions) form categories, and so the approach via categories fits well with the objective of organizing and understanding Mathematics. That, in truth, should be the goal of a proper philosophy of Mathematics.\n> \n> Mathematics: Form and Function (New York 1986).--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nMac Lane books - MacTutor History of Mathematics (https://mathshistory.st-andrews.ac.uk/Extras/MacLane_books/)\nciteturn809search10 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2.1 years ago; From the Preface. ... The subject came of age with the book by H Cartan and S Eilenberg (1956) which brought several trial theories within a single framework.This book is difficult to read: there is a mass of new ideas, the relative importance of which is not always clear; the writing is rather technical; and the presentation depends on some familiarity with modules over algebras, which is not really essential to the subject. ... Math. ... Mathematics, Form and Function (1985), by Saunders Mac Lane. ... Although motivated by philosophical concerns, the book is best read as a source for excellent descriptions of actual mathematical fields of practice, of the roots of the major problems that motivate many areas of research, and of revelations of many interconnections among the various branches of Mathematics.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nCelebratio Mathematica — Mac Lane — Bibliography (https://celebratio.org/MacLane_S/article/213/)\nciteturn809search11 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: yesterday;  1986 ... Mac Lane: Math­em­at­ics, form and func­tion. ...     \tTITLE = {Mathematics, form and function}, ... Mac Lane: “Con­cepts and cat­egor­ies in per­spect­ive,” pp. 323–​365 in A cen­tury of math­em­at­ics in Amer­ica, part I. ...     \tPUBLISHER = {American Mathematical Society},\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nMac Lane, Saunders (1986). *Mathematics: Form and Function*. Berlin: Springer. (https://ora.ox.ac.uk/objects/uuid%3A47784b87-7a7b-43c0-8ce2-8983a867d560/files/m4afb7fcaf0764448f75a9734a9ffca80)\nciteturn809search14 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 5 months ago; Mac Lane, Saunders (1986).*Mathematics: Form and Function*. ... ‘How We Learn Mathematical Language’.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nSaunders Mac Lane 1909-2005 (https://tholen.mathstats.yorku.ca/files/2021/01/Saunders-Mac-Lane.pdf?x32402=)\nciteturn809search15 [wordlim: 200] Published: 6 months ago; His 1986book “Mathematics, Form and Function” [MFF] describes much of his general